        "id": 1003065
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-10T12:39:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There is no specification present in Redfish for this: ServiceAddresses/Authentication.Username/LDAPService.SearchSettings and ExternalAccountProvider.ServiceEnabled is just documented as \"an indication of whether this service is enabled.\" This wasn\u0027t meant as a spec-compliance claim.\n\nThe intent is security hardening rather than spec conformance: once an LDAP provider is disabled, its server address and bind DN are stale config that\u0027s still exposed via GET to any authenticated client, even though the provider is no longer in use. Masking them while disabled reduces disclosure of internal directory infrastructure (server address, bind account) to clients that only need to know a provider exists and is off.",
      "parentUuid": "397d78f4_9180fcaa",
      "revId": "a2ffe3f211e7d2382f581514eeaad17fd228af78",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3d70ff96_0e55ad64",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 18,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000153
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-11T17:19:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e There is no specification present in Redfish for this:\n\nThen I have a strong preference to not change.\n\n\u003e Masking them while disabled reduces disclosure of internal directory infrastructure\n\nYou get the same result today by simply clearing the values.\n\n\u003e  to clients that only need to know a provider exists and is off\n\nThere is no distinction made to user classes for \"Can only know the provider is on or off\"\n\n\n\nI have a strong preference for not accepting this change.  First, security claim is dubious to me, and is trivially accomplished already if a client truly has these requirements of \"hiding\" the LDAP settings.\n\nSecond, I also would expect that it\u0027s rare to configure an LDAP/AD endpoint, then not use it as configured.\n\nThird, nowhere I\u0027m aware of declares a bind DN as a piece of confidential data.\n\nFinally, I\u0027m pretty sure this is a violation of the intent of Redfish, which is that the settings can be configured with the service disabled, THEN the service can be enabled.",
      "parentUuid": "15d26b8b_62215e04",
      "revId": "a2ffe3f211e7d2382f581514eeaad17fd228af78",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
